Subject: JSON.stringify writes single bytes inline, skips empty separators and indents, and remembers the shapes its walk has checked

What this does
  - `json_putc8`: string_buffer_putc8 with the common case (room left in an 8-bit buffer) inline, used by `json_quote_str` and `js_json_to_str`
  - with no gap, the indent of a nested level is the empty string itself (no concatenation per object or array) and the empty separators are not appended
  - `JSONStringifyContext.walk_ok`: 8 hashed shapes (held with a count, freed at the end) already found to have only string, non-index, non-varref keys, so the shape walk of 0585 checks each shape once per call

Why we need it
  Byte output through a called function, an empty-string concatenation per object and two empty appends per property, and the key-kind check of every object's shape were most of what 0585 left in stringify. json_app stringify only: -9.7% cycles, -18.1% instructions (after 0585).

Soundness
  The output bytes are unchanged (json_putc8 does what string_buffer_putc8 does; appending an empty string writes nothing; the indent with an empty gap is the indent). A hashed shape is shared and never edited in place, so a property list found suitable stays suitable while the shape is held; unhashed shapes are checked every time. Differentials of 0584/0585 (60,000 values with and without indentation, 25 hard cases incl. toJSON mutating the object; 1.5M doubles) are identical.

